###Pure

// valuation-core -- what a position is WORTH, and what that is worth in the currency the
// firm reports in. Layer 1: it depends on core-price (the price that goes into the
// valuation) and on core-fx (the rate that carries it into the reporting currency).
//
// The point of this project in the graph is the CROSS-PROJECT FUNCTION CALL. A valuation
// does not re-implement FX arithmetic; `VcValuation.valueInReporting()` is a derived
// property whose body is `core_fx::convert(...)`, so the conversion rule lives in exactly
// one place and every project above this one inherits it. Getting that call to resolve
// across a project boundary -- a function element in another package, invoked from inside
// a derived property body -- is the thing this project exists to prove.
//
// The second theme is the price stack, which is three numbers and not one:
//
//   cleanPrice        the quoted price, excluding interest earned since the last coupon
//   accruedInterest   that interest, per unit
//   dirtyPrice        clean + accrued -- what actually settles
//
// A bond quoted at 98.50 with 1.25 of accrued does not settle at 98.50, and a valuation
// that stores only "price" cannot say which of the two it means. So all three are carried,
// and `accrualCheck()` re-derives the accrued from the other two as a data-quality screen.
//
// Prefixes per the project contract: classes Vc*, tables VC_*, joins Vc_*, filters Vc*,
// set ids vc*, functions vc*.

// A single instrument's value on a single cob date, produced by a single valuation run --
// the grain of this project and the class every downstream project reaches for.
//
// Identity is the triple (runId, instrumentId, cobDate). All three are load-bearing: the
// same instrument on the same date is valued more than once, because the overnight run and
// the intraday re-run are different answers and a firm keeps both.
Class valuation_core::VcValuation
[
   // The dirty price includes accrued, so it can never sit below the clean price.
   dirtyCoversClean: $this.dirtyPrice >= $this.cleanPrice,
   // A negative accrual is a sign error, not a market event.
   accruedIsNotNegative: $this.accruedInterest >= 0.0,
   // A zero FX rate would silently zero every reported value downstream.
   fxRateIsPositive: $this.fxRate > 0.0
]
{
   // Part 1 of the key: which valuation run produced this number.
   runId: String[1];
   // Part 2 of the key: what was valued. Matches core-price's INSTRUMENT_ID.
   instrumentId: String[1];
   // Part 3 of the key: the close-of-business date valued as of.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // How many units are held. Signed: a short position is negative.
   quantity: Float[1];
   // The quoted price, EXCLUDING interest earned since the last coupon.
   cleanPrice: Float[1];
   // Interest earned since the last coupon, per unit. Zero for equities.
   accruedInterest: Float[1];
   // What actually settles: clean + accrued, per unit.
   dirtyPrice: Float[1];
   // quantity * dirtyPrice, in the INSTRUMENT's currency. Not yet comparable across a book.
   marketValue: Float[1];
   // ISO 4217 the marketValue is denominated in -- the instrument's own currency.
   instrumentCurrency: String[1];
   // ISO 4217 the firm reports in. Usually USD, and never assumed.
   reportingCurrency: String[1];
   // The pair the conversion is quoted on, e.g. EURUSD. Joins to core-fx.
   fxPairCode: String[1];
   // SPOT, FORWARD or FIXING -- part of core-fx's three-column rate key, so it must be
   // carried here or the join fans out across a day's rates.
   fxRateType: String[1];
   // The rate itself, already pointing instrument-currency -> reporting-currency. If the
   // pair is quotedInverted the run applied core_fx::invert before storing it.
   fxRate: Float[1];
   // Which core-price vendor's observation the price came from.
   priceSourceId: String[1];
   // False when the run could not find a usable price -- see VcUnpricedValuation.
   isPriced: Boolean[1];

   // ---- THE CROSS-PROJECT CALL ---------------------------------------------------------
   // The market value expressed in the reporting currency. The arithmetic is one multiply
   // and it still is not written here: core-fx owns conversion, this project calls it. Every
   // P&L, exposure and client statement above this layer reads this property, so the rule
   // it encodes is applied identically everywhere by construction.
   valueInReporting() { core_fx::convert($this.marketValue, $this.fxRate) } : Float[1];

   // The clean value -- the same conversion applied to the ex-interest leg, which is what a
   // bond desk quotes and what a coupon accrual report reconciles against.
   cleanValueInReporting() { core_fx::convert($this.cleanPrice * $this.quantity, $this.fxRate) } : Float[1];

   // The accrued leg alone, converted. valueInReporting minus this is cleanValueInReporting,
   // which is the identity an accrual break report checks.
   accruedValueInReporting() { core_fx::convert($this.accruedInterest * $this.quantity, $this.fxRate) } : Float[1];

   // Re-derive accrued from the two prices. Should equal accruedInterest; when it does not,
   // one of the three numbers was loaded from a different snapshot than the other two.
   accrualCheck() { $this.dirtyPrice - $this.cleanPrice } : Float[1];

   // Accrued as a fraction of the dirty price. `/` widens to Float even on integers, so this
   // is declared Float[1] as the contract requires.
   accruedPct() { $this.accruedInterest / $this.dirtyPrice } : Float[1];

   // The rate flipped, for a report that wants to state the value back in the instrument's
   // currency without re-reading the pair.
   inverseFxRate() { core_fx::invert($this.fxRate) } : Float[1];

   // ---- across the project boundary ----------------------------------------------------
   // The core-price observation this valuation priced off -- reached by the full
   // (instrument, date, source) triple, which is core-price's whole composite key.
   priceObservation: core_price::CprPriceObservation[0..1];
   // The core-fx rate row the fxRate was taken from -- reached by (pair, date, type),
   // which is core-fx's whole composite key.
   fxRateRow: core_fx::CfxRate[0..1];

   // ---- within this project --------------------------------------------------------------
   // The batch that produced this valuation.
   run: valuation_core::VcValuationRun[0..1];
   // The accrual behind accruedInterest, on instrument and date.
   accrual: valuation_core::VcAccrual[0..1];
   // The price the run chose, and what it was chosen over.
   priceInput: valuation_core::VcPriceInput[0..1];
   // Every adjustment stacked on this valuation, in no particular order -- the sequence
   // is a property of the adjustment, not of this link.
   adjustments: valuation_core::VcMarkAdjustment[*];
   // Every tolerance break raised against it.
   breaks: valuation_core::VcValuationBreak[*];
}

// The valuations a run could NOT price: same table, same key, filtered to the rows where
// isPriced is false. A named type rather than a predicate, because an unpriced valuation is
// an operational item with an owner, and downstream projects query for it directly.
Class valuation_core::VcUnpricedValuation extends valuation_core::VcValuation
{
   // Why no price was found: NO_SOURCE, STALE, NOT_COVERED, SUSPENDED.
   unpricedReason: String[0..1];
   // Whether the run fell back to the previous good price rather than leaving a hole.
   carriedForward: Boolean[0..1];
}

// One execution of the valuation process: a batch, its cob date, and whether it finished.
// Every VcValuation belongs to exactly one, which is what makes re-running a date safe.
Class valuation_core::VcValuationRun
{
   // The run identifier carried on every valuation it produced.
   runId: String[1];
   // The cob date the whole run values as of.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // ISO 4217 the run reports in -- fixed for the run, not per valuation.
   reportingCurrency: String[1];
   // EOD, INTRADAY or ADHOC. An intraday re-run does not replace the EOD run.
   runType: String[1];
   // PENDING, RUNNING, COMPLETE or FAILED.
   status: String[1];
   // How many valuations the run wrote.
   valuationCount: Integer[1];
   // How many it could not price. The pair of counts is the run's own quality metric.
   unpricedCount: Integer[1];
   // When the run started, to the second.
   startedAt: DateTime[1];
   // True once the run is signed off and may be reported from.
   isSignedOff: Boolean[1];
   // Coverage: the share of the run that got a price. Float, because it is a ratio.
   pricedPct() { ($this.valuationCount - $this.unpricedCount) / $this.valuationCount } : Float[1];
   // A one-line label for a report header, so the header does not concatenate it itself.
   runLabel() { $this.runType + ' ' + $this.status } : String[1];
   // The per-currency subtotals this run wrote.
   summaries: valuation_core::VcValuationSummary[*];
   // The currency the run reports in, with its rounding rule.
   reportingCurrencyRef: valuation_core::VcReportingCurrency[0..1];
}

// The interest accrual behind a valuation's accruedInterest: the coupon, the day count and
// the period it was earned over. Keyed by (instrumentId, cobDate) -- one accrual per
// instrument per day, independent of which run reads it.
Class valuation_core::VcAccrual
{
   // Which instrument is accruing.
   instrumentId: String[1];
   // The date accrued to.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Start of the current coupon period.
   periodStartDate: StrictDate[1];
   // End of it -- the next coupon date.
   periodEndDate: StrictDate[1];
   // The annual coupon rate as a fraction, e.g. 0.045 for 4.5%.
   couponRate: Float[1];
   // Face amount the coupon is computed on.
   notional: Float[1];
   // The day-count fraction for the elapsed part of the period, e.g. ACT/360.
   dayCountFraction: Float[1];
   // The day-count convention the fraction was computed under: ACT/360, ACT/365, 30/360.
   dayCountBasis: String[1];
   // The money accrued so far this period.
   accruedAmount: Float[1];
   // Accrued per unit of notional -- the number that lands on VcValuation.accruedInterest.
   accruedPerUnit() { $this.accruedAmount / $this.notional } : Float[1];
}

// How a value was arrived at: a quoted market price, a model, or a broker mark. The method
// decides what tolerance a break is measured against, so it is data and not a comment.
Class valuation_core::VcValuationMethod
{
   // Short code carried on a policy, e.g. MARK, MODEL, BROKER, MATRIX.
   methodCode: String[1];
   // What it is called in the valuation policy document.
   methodName: String[1];
   // True when the number comes from a model rather than an observed trade.
   isModelBased: Boolean[1];
   // True when an independent price verification team must sign the number off.
   requiresIpv: Boolean[1];
   // The percentage move that raises a break under this method. Model marks tolerate more.
   tolerancePct: Float[1];
   // Fair-value hierarchy level under IFRS 13: 1 quoted, 2 observable, 3 unobservable.
   fairValueLevel: Integer[1];
}

// The firm's exposure to one currency out of one run, and the same exposure carried into
// the reporting currency. This is the aggregate twin of VcValuation: valuations are per
// instrument, exposure is per currency, and both cross the FX boundary the same way.
Class valuation_core::VcFxExposure
{
   // Which run the exposure was measured in.
   runId: String[1];
   // The currency exposed to, ISO 4217.
   currencyCode: String[1];
   // The pair the exposure is converted on, in market convention order.
   fxPairCode: String[1];
   // Net exposure in that currency, signed.
   exposureAmount: Float[1];
   // The rate as the market quotes it -- NOT pre-flipped, unlike VcValuation.fxRate.
   fxRate: Float[1];
   // Copied from core-fx's pair definition: true when the market puts USD on the base side,
   // so the conversion has to invert before multiplying.
   quotedInverted: Boolean[1];
   // The reporting currency this exposure is measured into.
   reportingCurrency: String[1];
   // True when the exposure is inside the desk's standing FX limit.
   isWithinLimit: Boolean[1];

   // Convention-safe conversion, delegated whole to core-fx: convertQuoted decides whether
   // to invert from `quotedInverted` rather than making this project remember which of the
   // majors quotes USD as the base currency.
   exposureInReporting() { core_fx::convertQuoted($this.exposureAmount, $this.fxRate, $this.quotedInverted) } : Float[1];
   // The rate pointing the other way, for a report stating the reporting-currency amount
   // back in the exposed currency.
   inverseRate() { core_fx::invert($this.fxRate) } : Float[1];
   // core-fx's own definition of the pair -- the authority on `quotedInverted`, which this
   // class copies onto its row so a valuation run does not have to join to convert.
   pair: core_fx::CfxCurrencyPair[0..1];
}

// A manual adjustment applied on top of a computed valuation: a fair-value reserve, a
// liquidity haircut, a day-one P&L deferral. Keyed by (runId, instrumentId, adjustmentSeq)
// because several adjustments stack on one valuation and the order they were booked in is
// part of the audit trail.
Class valuation_core::VcMarkAdjustment
{
   // The run the adjustment belongs to.
   runId: String[1];
   // The valuation being adjusted.
   instrumentId: String[1];
   // Which adjustment this is, within the instrument and run. 1, 2, 3...
   adjustmentSeq: Integer[1];
   // RESERVE, HAIRCUT, DAY1, XVA, CLOSEOUT.
   adjustmentType: String[1];
   // The money added to the valuation, in the instrument's currency. Usually negative.
   adjustmentAmount: Float[1];
   // The rate to carry the adjustment into the reporting currency.
   fxRate: Float[1];
   // Free text: why a human overrode a computed number.
   reason: String[0..1];
   // Who signed it off. Absent while pending.
   approvedBy: String[0..1];
   // True once approved. An unapproved adjustment is excluded from reported value.
   isApproved: Boolean[1];
   // The adjustment in the reporting currency -- the same core-fx call the valuation uses,
   // so an adjustment and the value it adjusts are converted identically.
   adjustmentInReporting() { core_fx::convert($this.adjustmentAmount, $this.fxRate) } : Float[1];
}

// A tolerance break: the run's value disagreed with an independent check by more than the
// method allows. Keyed by (runId, instrumentId, breakSeq) -- one valuation can break more
// than one check.
Class valuation_core::VcValuationBreak
{
   // The run that raised it.
   runId: String[1];
   // The instrument whose value broke.
   instrumentId: String[1];
   // Which break this is for that instrument in that run.
   breakSeq: Integer[1];
   // What broke: PRICE_TOL, FX_TOL, STALE_PRICE, NO_CONSENSUS, ACCRUAL_MISMATCH.
   breakCode: String[1];
   // 1 (informational) to 5 (blocks sign-off).
   severity: Integer[1];
   // The absolute difference, in the reporting currency.
   differenceAmount: Float[1];
   // The same difference as a fraction of the value -- what the tolerance is stated in.
   differencePct: Float[1];
   // When it was raised.
   raisedAt: DateTime[1];
   // True once someone has explained or corrected it.
   isResolved: Boolean[1];
}

// The rule that decides where a price comes from for a class of instruments: which vendor,
// which method, and how old a price may be before it stops counting. Keyed by policyCode.
Class valuation_core::VcPricingPolicy
{
   // The policy identifier, e.g. GOVT_BOND_EOD.
   policyCode: String[1];
   // What it applies to: BOND, EQUITY, FUTURE, SWAP, FUND.
   assetClass: String[1];
   // The VcValuationMethod to use.
   methodCode: String[1];
   // The core-price vendor to prefer.
   preferredSourceId: String[1];
   // How stale a price may be, in business days, before the valuation is marked unpriced.
   maxPriceAgeDays: Integer[1];
   // The tolerance override for this asset class, when it differs from the method's.
   tolerancePct: Float[1];
   // True when this is the fallback for an asset class with no more specific policy.
   isDefault: Boolean[1];
   // The method this policy names.
   method: valuation_core::VcValuationMethod[0..1];
   // The core-price vendor it prefers -- across the boundary, into the other project.
   preferredSource: core_price::CprPriceSource[0..1];
}

// A currency the firm can report in, and how values are rounded when it does. Separate from
// core-price's quote currency: a price currency is what the market quotes, a reporting
// currency is what a set of books is added up in.
Class valuation_core::VcReportingCurrency
{
   // ISO 4217, and the key.
   currencyCode: String[1];
   // The name as it appears on a statement.
   currencyName: String[1];
   // Decimal places a reported value is rounded to: 2 for USD, 0 for JPY.
   roundingDecimals: Integer[1];
   // The pair quoted against the firm's base currency, e.g. EURUSD for EUR.
   basePairCode: String[1];
   // True for the firm's own base currency -- exactly one row should have it.
   isFirmBase: Boolean[1];
   // core-fx's definition of the pair this currency is quoted against the firm's base on.
   basePair: core_fx::CfxCurrencyPair[0..1];
}

// A run's total, per currency: the aggregate that a P&L or client-reporting project reads
// instead of summing every valuation itself. Keyed by (runId, currencyCode).
Class valuation_core::VcValuationSummary
{
   // The run summarised.
   runId: String[1];
   // The instrument currency the subtotal is for.
   currencyCode: String[1];
   // The sum of marketValue over that currency, in that currency.
   totalMarketValue: Float[1];
   // The rate used to carry the whole subtotal across.
   fxRate: Float[1];
   // How many valuations went into it.
   positionCount: Integer[1];
   // How many of them were unpriced -- the subtotal is only as good as this is small.
   unpricedCount: Integer[1];
   // The subtotal in the reporting currency, via core-fx. Converting the total once is not
   // the same as summing converted values when rates differ per row, which is why the
   // summary carries its own rate.
   totalInReporting() { core_fx::convert($this.totalMarketValue, $this.fxRate) } : Float[1];
   // Average value per position, for a size sanity check on the run.
   averagePositionValue() { $this.totalMarketValue / $this.positionCount } : Float[1];
}

// The price actually used by a run, recorded next to the alternatives it was chosen over.
// Keyed by (runId, instrumentId). This is where core-price's two-sided quote lands: a mid
// is not stored, it is derived from the bid and ask with core-fx's midOf.
Class valuation_core::VcPriceInput
{
   // The run that selected this price.
   runId: String[1];
   // The instrument it was selected for.
   instrumentId: String[1];
   // The core-price vendor it came from.
   sourceId: String[1];
   // The price the run used, in the instrument's currency.
   priceUsed: Float[1];
   // The vendor's bid at selection time.
   bidPrice: Float[1];
   // The vendor's ask at selection time.
   askPrice: Float[1];
   // ISO 4217 the price is quoted in.
   priceCurrency: String[1];
   // How many business days old the price was when used. 0 is same-day.
   priceAgeDays: Integer[1];
   // True when the run took a cross-vendor consensus rather than one vendor's number.
   isConsensus: Boolean[1];
   // The mid of the two-way price, from core-fx's helper rather than re-written here --
   // every project that writes ($bid + $ask) / 2.0 itself is one that can write it wrong.
   midPrice() { core_fx::midOf($this.bidPrice, $this.askPrice) } : Float[1];
   // The spread the run accepted, as a fraction of the mid.
   spreadPct() { ($this.askPrice - $this.bidPrice) / $this.priceUsed } : Float[1];
   // The core-price vendor the price came from.
   source: core_price::CprPriceSource[0..1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ exported functions
// The valuation arithmetic downstream projects (pnl-attribution, product-pricing,
// client-reporting) call rather than re-derive. Two of the four are thin wrappers over
// core-fx: the cross-project call again, this time at function level rather than inside a
// derived property.

// The dirty price: what settles, as opposed to what is quoted. This is the definition the
// whole project rests on, and it is one line precisely so nobody writes it differently.
function valuation_core::vcDirtyPrice(cleanPrice: Float[1], accruedInterest: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   $cleanPrice + $accruedInterest
}

// Market value in the instrument's own currency: units times what one unit settles at.
function valuation_core::vcMarketValue(quantity: Float[1], dirtyPrice: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   $quantity * $dirtyPrice
}

// Market value in the reporting currency, in one call. Delegates the conversion to core-fx
// so that this project never multiplies by an FX rate itself.
function valuation_core::vcMarketValueInReporting(quantity: Float[1], dirtyPrice: Float[1], fxRate: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   core_fx::convert(valuation_core::vcMarketValue($quantity, $dirtyPrice), $fxRate)
}

// The whole valuation in one call, convention-safe: clean and accrued in, reporting-currency
// value out, with core-fx deciding from `quotedInverted` which way the rate points. This is
// the entry point a downstream project should prefer.
function valuation_core::vcValueQuoted(quantity: Float[1], cleanPrice: Float[1], accruedInterest: Float[1], fxRate: Float[1], quotedInverted: Boolean[1]): Float[1]
{
   core_fx::convertQuoted(valuation_core::vcMarketValue($quantity, valuation_core::vcDirtyPrice($cleanPrice, $accruedInterest)), $fxRate, $quotedInverted)
}

// The difference between two valuations of the same thing, as a fraction of the first --
// what a tolerance in VcValuationMethod.tolerancePct is compared against.
function valuation_core::vcDeviationPct(valueOne: Float[1], valueTwo: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   ($valueTwo - $valueOne) / $valueOne
}
